A||||5||PatrickB||No juice||||8||
Z||000000||PatrickB||10-17-2000||02:27 PM||||I need your help with this one because I cant stand electrical problems.  Heres the deal:  Since I've had my car it would always do this thing whenever I pushed the brake pedal or used the turn signal.  When I pressed on the brakes the voltmeter would go down a bit and the headlights would dim.  When I used the turn signal, the voltmeter would jump around to the rythym of the turn signal.  Now last night when I was driving whenever I hit the brake or used the turn signal I would lose power for about a second.  My radio would go completely dead because I lost the presets.  After a bit it was okay and then it started doing it this morning.  So when I get home I decide to take a look at it.  I go to turn on the ignition and I get nothing.  I am not getting any power at all.  The alarm on my car still gets power and works fine though.<BR>I installed a new alternator a few days ago in hopes of fixing this problem but it did not help.  The alternator has been working just as it should be now.<P>Anyone have any ideas?  I am going out to look at it now.<P>Pat||||reg||8
Z||000001||PatrickB||10-17-2000||05:45 PM||||Ok I actually fixed it myself.  Kind of suprised.  What happened was the little ground wire that goes just below the battery was loose and corroded.  Im guessing(and correct me if Im wrong) that this ground wire is what grounds the harness and the ecm.<BR>Anyhow, I have power back but I am still having the problem with when I use the brakes or the turn signal the power goes out for a second and than comes back on.  Anyone have any ideas?  Another bad ground somewhere?<P>Thanks,<BR> Pat||||reg||1
Z||000002||Spyro86SE||10-17-2000||05:49 PM||||hey i have the same problem i changed my alt.<BR>and still have the problem have your battery tested it might be going bad and have the alt tested that might be going bad too do you have the 2.8 or 2.5 let me know so i can check my wire to||||reg||11
Z||000003||Mark||10-17-2000||06:27 PM||||Check the ground braid between the block and the lower passenger-side nut on the deck lid hinge.  If this is loose or corroded, you will see a noticable voltage drop whenever you use turn signals, defroster blower, etc.  For that matter, any electrical connection, including the battery terminals, should be checked.||||reg||1
Z||000004||PatrickB||10-17-2000||07:17 PM||||Spyro86SE,  <BR>I thought it would be my alternator and I had  gotten a new one a while back so I threw it in.  While there is a definate increase in power it didnt help my problem.  The battery is about 6 months old but I am going to get it checked for a bad cell anyway.<P>Mark,<BR> Are you referring to the copper braided wire  over there?  I know one part connects to the lower passenger-side nut on the deck lid but does the other part connect to one of the screws just below it on the firewall or does it connect somewhere on the block?  Mine  is on a screw on the firewall. It was actually not connected and I just found it folded up and tucked away so I re-connected it.<P>Also, on the other side there is a thin black ground wire connected to the driver side of the decklid but is not connected to anything else.  Anyone know where this goes?  I have to find out what the original owner was doing with all these grounds.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||ka4nkf||10-17-2000||09:04 PM||||The braided wire connects to the trunk hinge bolt and the other end connects to the engine I think maybe on the exhaust manifold stud, or any bolt that you find on the engine. The black wire on drivers side connects to the trunk hinge bolt and then to a  bolt on the transmission. remember the more grounds you have, the better you are.||||reg||1
